# =====================================================================================================================================================
# square/frontend/.env.example   -   the SQUARE SITE (what visitors of a square see: <slug>.rootsquares.com; Vite, port 5274 locally)
# Written for W26-R52 (2026-11-03). Every name below is read in square/frontend/src. Full reference: docs/setup/13-environment-variables.md.
# =====================================================================================================================================================
#
# EVERY VITE_ VALUE IS PUBLIC (written into the page at build time). Never put a secret here. A changed value reaches the site only after a NEW BUILD:
# restart `npm run dev`, or redeploy the static site on Render.
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   1. Make the file for your environment by copying this one (all three are git-ignored, none is ever committed):
#        .env.local        your machine. Vite reads it for `npm run dev` and for any build on this machine.
#        .env.staging      a notebook for the Render STAGING static site (guide 41 Step 7), and the file `--mode staging` uses on this machine.
#        .env.production   the same for production.
#      Render never sees these files. On Render you paste the lines into the static site's Environment tab.
#   2. Build or run on this machine with another file (PowerShell, from square\frontend):
#        npm run dev -- --mode staging
#        npm run build -- --mode staging
#      Vite reads .env, .env.local, .env.staging (the later wins; .env.staging overrides .env.local, but a name it lacks falls through to .env.local, so put every name you need in it).
#      A value already in the shell or in Render's dashboard always wins over every file.
#
# HOW THIS SITE FINDS ITS SERVER: one server serves every square and picks the square from the HOST NAME of the request. So the site calls /api on ITS OWN address and the host name travels with it:
# locally Vite forwards /api to port 5002 and keeps the host name (vite.config.ts); on Render the static site rewrites /api/* to the square server (guide 41 Step 7, the Host test).
#
# WHERE:  L = .env.local   S = .env.staging   P = .env.production      req = required   opt = optional

# VITE_API_BASE_URL   WHERE: L=req (empty)   S=req (empty)   P=req (empty)
# LEAVE THE VALUE EMPTY ON PURPOSE: that makes the site call /api on its own address. A full address here would send the browser to another host and the server could no longer tell which square is meant.
# The line must exist (an unset name makes the code build a wrong address); keep it as `VITE_API_BASE_URL=` with nothing after the =.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# VITE_AUTH_PROVIDER / VITE_AUTH_URL / VITE_AUTH_PUBLIC_KEY   WHERE: L=opt   S=opt   P=opt
# FALLBACK ONLY. A served square tells the browser its own sign-in project (the square server answers it from the platform database), and that answer wins. These three are used for a
# single-tenant server and for the test suite. If neither the square's answer nor this fallback gives a provider and a URL, the site stops at load with a clear message.
#   supabase | https://<project-ref>.supabase.co | the publishable key (sb_publishable_...)      or      neon | the Neon Auth URL | no key
VITE_AUTH_PROVIDER=supabase
VITE_AUTH_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
VITE_AUTH_PUBLIC_KEY=<sb_publishable_...>
